{"clock":1,"nonce":"47644a147362adc959066a8e03d069db","ops":[{"kind":"create","author":"61b55b322674a787b89ef011a06fca88d5ce49b7","time":1794314116,"origin":{"forge":"github.com/guilbep/bimble","event":"issue:6306910066"},"data":{"body":"The desktop opens 5 and 7 GB IFC files (ADR 0039). The browser doesn't: a tab holds at most 4 GB (wasm32), and a picked file is read whole into memory (`Source::Bytes`); the parse alone is about 5 × the displayed bytes.\n\nTo do, in order of cost:\n- [ ] Stream the file: `Blob.slice()` windows in a worker with `FileReaderSync`, fed to `parse_read_skipping` (already takes any `Read`)\n- [ ] Parse less: an offset index, geometry parsed per element, storey or cell (as the census does)\n- [ ] Point large IFCs to the prepared format (`.bimbleview`, ADR 0040, `bimble pack` / Share for viewing); far cells as proxies first, the rest on demand\n- [ ] Later: wasm64 (memory64) once wgpu and wasm-bindgen support it\n- [ ] Measure on the synthetic repeated test files\n\nSee docs/adr/0039-open-5-gb-ifc-files.md, section \"The browser (wasm32): not built here\".","title":"Browser: open 5 GB IFC files (ADR 0039)"}}]}